Overview StackStream Bounty Board is a user-facing marketplace on Stacks that connects builders, DAOs, protocols, and companies to StackStream's streaming-payment infrastructure. Instead of paying bounties, grants, or program stipends as lump sums, funders deposit into the board and payouts flow to selected builders as a continuous stream over a set duration — using StackStream's existing start/pause/stop primitives, with no new streaming logic required on StackStream's side. The board supports two funding models:

Task/Bounty Stream — a single deliverable, a set price, a set duration, streamed to one builder.
Cohort Accelerator Program — a GoodDollar-style multi-project growth program, where a small group of already-live Stacks products receive a streamed stipend over a fixed period in exchange for hitting weekly growth targets, with underperforming projects dropped along the way. The goal is to give the Stacks ecosystem a reusable front end for StackStream that works equally well for a one-week freelance task and a two-month ecosystem growth program. Product Types
Task/Bounty Stream Cohort Accelerator Program Who posts Any founder, DAO, protocol, or company Program committee (curated intake) Who receives One builder or small team 5 pre-selected, already-live Stacks products Duration Founder-set (e.g. 1 week) Fixed program length (e.g. 2 months) Amount Founder-set (e.g. $10–100) Pooled stipend per project (e.g. $100–300 total) Success metric Deliverable submitted and approved Weekly growth metrics (onboarding, retention, referrals) Stream control Start on selection, stop/pause on completion or dispute Start on cohort kickoff, paused or stopped on committee vote to drop Review cadence On submission Weekly report + weekly committee meeting Both types share the same underlying escrow-and-stream infrastructure and the same StackStream tunnel layer — they differ only in posting flow, review cadence, and stream-control triggers, so they can ship as two front-end flows over one backend. Founder Flow Task/Bounty Stream

Connect wallet, create a bounty: title, description, deliverable criteria, amount, duration.
Deposit funds into board escrow.
Review applicants (or accept the first qualified applicant, board's choice).
Select a builder — board triggers a StackStream stream to the builder's wallet for the set duration.
Receive progress updates during the duration; approve final deliverable.
On approval, stream completes naturally (or founder can pause/stop early if work stalls). Cohort Accelerator Program
Committee (which may include the founder posting the program) defines: program length, total pool, number of slots (e.g. 5), success metrics (users onboarded, retention delta, referral activity).
Open or invite-based intake; committee selects the cohort.
Program kicks off — StackStream streams start for all selected projects simultaneously.
Each week, committee reviews submitted reports and votes on any projects to flag or drop.
On a drop decision, that project's stream is stopped; remaining pool is either redistributed to surviving projects or reserved for the next cohort round (decision needed — see Open Questions).
Program ends with surviving projects having received their full stream; committee documents outcomes for the ecosystem. Builder Flow Task/Bounty Stream
Browse open bounties, filter by amount, duration, or skill tag.
Apply with a short proposal (or claim directly, if the bounty is first-come).
On selection, StackStream stream to their wallet begins automatically for the bounty's duration.
Submit progress updates and the final deliverable through the board.
On founder approval, stream completes; builder's board profile records the completed bounty (reputation history). Cohort Accelerator Program
Apply to the cohort (or get invited, if intake is curated) with product details and current traction.
If selected, stream begins at cohort kickoff for the full program duration.
Each week: submit a structured report — users onboarded, retention/activity delta, what shipped, next week's plan — before the weekly committee meeting.
Attend the weekly review; present results and answer committee questions.
If flagged (metrics not growing), get one week to course-correct before a second review.
If dropped after the second flagged week, stream stops and the project exits the cohort. If retained, continue through to the end of the program. Application & Selection Flow flowchart LR A[Founder/Committee
posts bounty or cohort] --> B[Deposit funds
into board escrow] B --> C[Builders/Projects
apply] C --> D{Selection} D -->|Chosen| E[StackStream stream
starts to wallet] D -->|Not chosen| F[Applicant notified] E --> G[Progress updates /
weekly reports] G --> H{Review} H -->|On track| G H -->|Complete| I[Stream ends naturally] H -->|Dropped/failed| J[Stream paused or stopped] One reading: both product types share this same spine — post, deposit, apply, select, stream, review, end — with the review loop (G/H) running once at submission for a bounty, and weekly for a cohort. Committee & Review Process All drop/continue decisions are made by a human committee, never by the platform automatically. The board's role is to surface data (reports, metrics) and execute the committee's decision (start/pause/stop the stream) — it does not decide. Weekly report (per project, submitted before the weekly meeting): • Users onboarded this week (number) • Retention/activity delta vs. prior week • What shipped • Next week's plan Two-strike drop mechanism:
Week N: committee reviews the report. Metrics flat or declining → project is flagged, given one week to course-correct.
Week N+1: committee reviews again. Improved → flag cleared, project continues normally. Still underperforming → committee votes.
On a drop vote: stream is stopped, project exits the cohort. This protects a team from being cut over one rough week (illness, a pivot in progress) while keeping real pressure on sustained non-performance. The report itself becomes the audit trail behind every drop decision, so disputes have a record to point to rather than relying on what was said in a call. StackStream Integration The board is a client of StackStream's existing infrastructure — it introduces no new streaming primitives. Every stream the board creates uses only: • Start — triggered the moment a builder is selected (bounty) or a cohort kicks off, parameterized by the amount and duration set at posting. • Pause — triggered by a committee vote to flag a project, or by a founder pausing a bounty mid-way. • Stop — triggered by a committee drop vote, founder cancellation, or natural completion at the end of the set duration. The board's job is to hold escrowed deposits, map board-side decisions (selection, approval, committee votes) onto these three StackStream calls, and index stream status back into the UI so founders, builders, and the committee can see progress at a glance. No rate-adjustment or event-gated streaming is needed — confirmed as out of scope after walking through the cohort mechanic; start/pause/stop covers every case in this PRD. Since there's a direct line to the StackStream founder, the integration can likely be a straightforward API/webhook connection rather than a from-scratch contract integration — exact shape (does the board initialize streams itself, or request StackStream to do so on the board's behalf) is one of the first things to align on. Worked Example: 5 Products → 3 Survivors An illustrative 8-week cohort, $300 total pool per project, $37.50/week baseline stream: Project Week 1–2 Week 3–4 Week 5–6 Week 7–8 Outcome A Growing steadily Growing Growing Growing Graduates, full $300 streamed B Growing Flat (flagged wk4) Recovered Growing Graduates, full $300 streamed C Growing Growing Flat (flagged wk6) Still flat (dropped wk7) Dropped, ~$225 streamed before stop D Flat from week 1 (flagged wk2) Still flat (dropped wk3) — — Dropped early, $75 streamed before stop E Growing Growing Growing Growing Graduates, full $300 streamed Result: 5 products enter, 3 (A, B, E) graduate with a full 8-week stream, 2 (C, D) are cut by committee vote after failing to recover within their one-week grace period. Unstreamed funds from C and D ($300 combined) can either top up a graduation bonus for A/B/E, or roll into the next cohort's pool — a decision the committee should set as policy before the program launches, not case-by-case. The same mechanic applies whether the metric is user onboarding (as above), a founder-set task deliverable (bounty version, single checkpoint at submission), or a longer growth-style program — the board's report-and-vote loop doesn't change, only what counts as "growing." Open Questions (for StackStream founder discussion) [ ] Integration shape: does the board initialize StackStream streams directly (board holds the trigger), or does the board request StackStream to initialize on its behalf (StackStream holds the trigger)? [ ] Escrow custody: while a bounty is open (pre-selection) or during committee review, do funds sit in a board-side escrow contract, or does StackStream expect to hold deposits itself? [ ] Dropped-project funds: does the unstreamed remainder from a dropped cohort project roll into a graduation bonus for survivors, or reserve for the next cohort round — committee should set this as policy pre-launch. [ ] MVP scope: launch with both Task/Bounty Stream and Cohort Accelerator Program, or ship the simpler bounty flow first and add the cohort flow once the tunnel layer is proven? [ ] Builder reputation: should completed bounties/cohorts build a visible on-board reputation history, to help founders choose applicants on future bounties? [ ] Does StackStream's current tooling expose stream status (started/paused/stopped, amount disbursed so far) in a form the board can index directly, or does the board need to track this itself?
